rity of observations (Q1, Median, Q3 all = 0) and provides minimal variation across the dataset, reducing its utility for predictions.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column code_and_comment_ratio is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['code_and_comment_ratio'], inplace=True)\n# The code_and_comment_ratio has a mean of close to 0 and exhibits very little relationship with the defect prediction target variable, which implies it does not drive meaningful predictions.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column log_branchCount is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['log_branchCount'], inplace=True)\n# The branchCount variable itself, already part of the dataset, provides all the necessary information. The log-transformation does not enhance the interaction with the defectiveness target variable and can lead to redundancy.\n```end", "```python\n# 1. cyclomatic_complexity_density\n# cyclomatic_complexity_density = v(g) / (loc + 1)\n# Usefulness: This feature measures how complex the code is relative to its size (line count). Modules with higher cyclomatic complexity density are likely harder to test/maintain and thus more defect-prone. This aligns with the relationship between complexity and software quality described in the dataset.\n\ndf['cyclomatic_complexity_density'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['loc'] + 1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# 2. defect_proneness_index\n# defect_proneness_index = (ev(g) + iv(g)) / (uniq_Opnd + uniq_Op + 1)\n# Usefulness: This feature combines essential and design complexity with unique operators and operands, representing how error-prone a module could be based on both complexity and structural factors. Defect prediction often relies on identifying the balance between complexity and resource utilization.\n\ndf['defect_proneness_index'] = (df['ev(g)'] + df['iv(g)']) / (df['uniq_Opnd'] + df['uniq_Op'] + 1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# 3. cognitive_load\n# cognitive_load = log_v * (log_branchCount + 1) / (log_n + 1)\n# Usefulness: Cognitive load is a proxy combining Halstead's volume and branching structure. A higher cognitive load indicates that a module may be more difficult for developers to process and understand, creating higher chances for defects.\n\ndf['cognitive_load'] = df['log_v'] * (df['log_branchCount'] + 1) / (df['log_n'] + 1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping columns not improving informativeness\n# Explanation why the column blank_line_density is dropped\n# Redundant information: The blank_line_density column provides minimal signal to defect prediction as it does not directly influence the factors associated with software quality like complexity or operations.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['blank_line_density'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column code_and_comment_ratio is dropped\n# Redundant information: The code_and_comment_ratio column is not significantly differentiating between defect and non-defect modules based on its low variability (Mean ≈ 0.0059, Median = 0). Other commenting-related metrics capture the signal better.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['code_and_comment_ratio'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column comment_density is dropped\n# Redundant feature: Similar reasoning as blank_line_density and code_and_comment_ratio. Comment density does not provide explicit complexity/defect information. Also, the median and most data points are 0, making it less useful.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['comment_density'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
