ore" provides an easily interpretable signal of defect likelihood and reduces multi-collinearity issues from individual variables.\ndf[\'defect_proneness_score\'] = 0.5 * df[\'ev(g)\'] + 0.3 * df[\'iv(g)\'] + 0.2 * df[\'branchCount\']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column \'log_branchCount\' is dropped\n# The column `log_branchCount` has very low variability (Mean = 1.2951, Std = 0.8361). Its distribution shows most values are clustered near the minimum, which reduces its utility for distinguishing defect-prone modules.\n# Additionally, its log transformation does not provide any substantial improvement over the raw `branchCount` column. We prioritize raw counts or combined features over redundant logarithmic transformations.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'log_branchCount\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column \'code_and_comment_ratio\' is dropped\n# The column `code_and_comment_ratio` has a very narrow range with most values equaling 0, as evidenced by Q1, Median, and Q3 all being 0. This means it has almost no discriminative power.\n# Moreover, the lack of comments in this data suggests that commentary information is unreliable for this specific dataset, and other density measures like `comment_density` already capture similar information (if any) more effectively.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'code_and_comment_ratio\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column \'log_e\' is dropped\n# The `log_e` column (log-transformed Halstead effort) is highly correlated with its original value `e` (effort). Keeping both introduces redundancy into the model, increasing dimensionality without adding new information.\n# As the use case for log-transformed scaling doesn\'t provide an apparent advantage (effort itself is interpretable as a raw measure), we retain the original `e` for the model while dropping `log_e`.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'log_e\'], inplace=True)\n```end', "```python\n# Step 1: Generate interaction features representing more complex relationships\n# Feature name: loc_d_interaction\n# Feature description: This feature captures the interaction between `loc` (lines of code) and `d` (Halstead difficulty). It is computed as `loc * d`.\n# Usefulness: Defect-prone modules may potentially have high difficulty (d) when paired with complex codebases (loc). By explicitly capturing this interaction, the model might better differentiate between complex, defect-prone modules and those that are simple and less error-prone.\ndf['loc_d_interaction'] = df['loc'] * df['d']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Create a feature representing code modularity and design complexity\n# Feature name: modularity_ratio\n# Feature description: This feature is computed as `iv(g) / (v(g) + 1)`, which captures the design complexity `iv(g)` relative to cyclomatic complexity `v(g)`.\n# Usefulness: `iv(g)` and `v(g)` both measure structural complexity, but the ratio might help the model better distinguish tightly coupled or modular code. Poor modularity is often linked with a higher chance of defects in software engineering.\ndf['modularity_ratio'] = df['iv(g)'] / (df['v(g)'] + 1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Concatenate logarithmic and interaction-based features to enhance interpretability\n# Feature name: log_loc_vg\n# Feature description: This uses the log of the already computed interaction feature `loc_vg_interaction` for normalization and neural sensitivity.\n# Usefulness: The log transformation ensures the feature is rescaled, which may help learning algorithms better handle the long-tailed distribution of `loc_vg_interaction`. Large interaction magnitudes in untransformed data can dominate the model’s learning and lead to poor generalization.\ndf['log_loc_vg'] = np.log1p(df['loc_vg_interaction'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why `comment_density` is dropped\n# Although interesting, comment density is largely zero for most data points (Q1, Median, Q3 are all 0 in its distribution). It likely provides minimal variation for training and unnecessarily increases dimensionality.\ndf.drop(columns=['comment_density'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why `blank_line_density` is dropped\n# Similar to `comment_density`, the Q1, Median, and Q3 values suggest most data points have negligible blank line density. It is likely uninformative for learning and redundant noise for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['blank_line_density'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why `code_and_comment_ratio` is dropped\n# This feature describes the ratio of code to comments and is constant for most samples. Furthermore, its values are zero for the majority of the dataset (Mean = 0.0059), showing limited utility for predictive modeling.\ndf.drop(columns=['code_and_comment_ratio'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
